The problem asks us to simulate the process of aliens becoming adults and receiving stones from other adults over $N$ years.
There are $N$ aliens. The $i$-th alien (1-indexed) starts with $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When the $i$-th alien becomes an adult (at year $i$), every adult who currently has at least 1 stone gives exactly 1 stone to the $i$-th alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.

Let's trace the process.
At year $i$ (for $i=1, \dots, N$), the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
Before year $i$, the $i$-th alien was a minor.
At year $i$, the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
The rule says: "every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
Who are the adults at year $i$?
The aliens who have become adults are $1, 2, \dots, i-1$.
Wait, the problem says "When someone becomes an adult... every adult who has at least one stone gives...".
Does the newly adult alien count as an adult *before* receiving the gift?
Usually, "becomes an adult" implies the transition happens. The gift is given *to* the alien who just became an adult. So the set of givers are the adults existing before this event.
Let's check the sample explanation to clarify.
