Let's look at the sample explanation carefully.
"After 1 year, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (5, 0, 9, 3)."
This implies Alien 1 received 0 stones.
"After 2 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (4, 1, 9, 3)."
Alien 2 becomes adult.
Adults before year 2: Alien 1.
Alien 1 has 5 stones. Since $5 \ge 1$, Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 2.
Alien 1 loses 1 stone: $5 \to 4$.
Alien 2 gains 1 stone: $0 \to 1$.
Result: $[4, 1, 9, 3]$. Correct.
"After 3 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (3, 0, 11, 3)."
Alien 3 becomes adult.
Adults before year 3: Alien 1, Alien 2.
Alien 1 has 4 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1. $4 \to 3$.
Alien 2 has 1 stone ($\ge 1$). Gives 1. $1 \to 0$.
Alien 3 gains $1+1=2$ stones. $9 \to 11$.
Result: $[3, 0, 11, 3]$. Correct.
"After 4 years, (C_1, C_2, C_3, C_4) = (2, 0, 10, 5)."
Alien 4 becomes adult.
Adults before year 4: Alien 1, Alien 2, Alien 3.
Alien 1 has 3 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1. $3 \to 2$.
Alien 2 has 0 stones ($< 1$). Gives 0. $0 \to 0$.
Alien 3 has 11 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1. $11 \to 10$.
Alien 4 gains $1+0+1=2$ stones. $3 \to 5$.
Result: $[2, 0, 10, 5]$. Correct.

